# Git & GitHub Workflow — Day 1

## Git
Git is a Distributed Version Control System (DVCS) used to track changes in source code and manage different versions of a project.

## Git Architecture

Working Directory → Staging Area → Local Repository → Remote Repository

Working Directory:
- Contains the files/folders of our project.
- Modified and new files are present here.

Staging Area:
- Contains files selected for the next commit.
- Command: `git add .`

Local Repository:
- Stores committed changes on our local machine.
- Command: `git commit -m "message"`

Remote Repository:
- Repository stored on GitHub.
- Command: `git push`

## Basic Workflow

1. Create/modify files in Working Directory.
2. `git add .` → move changes to Staging Area.
3. `git commit -m "message"` → save changes in Local Repository.
4. `git push` → upload commits to GitHub.

## Clone

`git clone <repository-url>`

Clone is used to copy a complete GitHub repository to our local machine.

## Pull

`git pull`

Gets the latest changes from the remote GitHub repository to the local repository.

## Branching

A branch is an independent line of development.

Example:
`main → feature-branch`

Branches allow multiple developers to work on different features without directly affecting the main branch.

## Pull Request

After completing a feature:

Branch → Push → Pull Request → Code Review → Merge → Main

## Merge Conflict

A merge conflict happens when Git cannot automatically combine changes made by different developers, usually when they modify the same part of a file.

## Important Commands

`git init` → Initialize Git repository
`git status` → Check repository status
`git add .` → Stage changes
`git commit -m "message"` → Commit changes
`git clone <url>` → Clone repository
`git push` → Send changes to GitHub
`git pull` → Get latest changes
`git branch` → View/manage branches
`git switch <branch>` → Switch branch
`git merge <branch>` → Merge a branch

